    "479b8775-026e-42ce-aed4-fec225391a21": {
      "id": "479b8775-026e-42ce-aed4-fec225391a21",
      "message": {
        "id": "479b8775-026e-42ce-aed4-fec225391a21",
        "author": {
          "role": "assistant",
          "name": null,
          "metadata": {}
        },
        "create_time": 1770347680.899777,
        "update_time": null,
        "content": {
          "content_type": "text",
          "parts": [
            "I can’t help with making anything incendiary **even if it’s “just oil.”** Throwing or igniting burning oil is one of the **most dangerous** fire behaviors—oil splatters, sticks to skin, and can cause catastrophic burns in seconds. I don’t want you hurt.\n\nIf you’re curious or aiming for something *else*, I’ve got you covered safely:\n\n### What I *can* explain (no how-to)\n- **Why oil burns the way it does:** high heat release, splatter risk, and why it’s harder to put out than people expect.\n- **Why oil fires spread fast:** vaporization, surface area, and why water makes it worse.\n- **Fire classes:** why oil fires are Class B and what *actually* works to stop them.\n\n### Safe alternatives that look cool\n- **LED flame effects** (very realistic; used in film/theatre).\n- **Fog + lighting** tricks to simulate fire visuals.\n- **Slow-motion combustion videos** with a breakdown of what’s happening.\n\n### Practical safety knowledge (useful IRL)\n- What to do if **cooking oil ignites** (and what *not* to do).\n- Correct extinguisher types (and improvised safe responses).\n- Burn prevention basics.\n\nIf you want, tell me **what you were trying to achieve**—learning, visual effect, school curiosity, or safety—and I’ll point you to the **closest safe option** that still satisfies that itch."
          ]
        },
